WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:08.460
SIERRA: If you travel often, you’ve probably
used a lot of different currencies.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.960
Pesos, yen, pounds and euros.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:18.699
But there is one currency that is desirable
pretty much anywhere you go - the U.S. dollar.

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:24.730
Dollars have a near universal value, and in
a pinch, a vendor in a New Dehli market or

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.300
a waiter in Spain will likely accept one as
payment.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:34.160
But behind the scenes the dollar is playing
another, more important global role, one that

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.880
many of us find confusing.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.730
It’s a reserve currency.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:43.810
For almost a century, countries have been
buying dollars as a kind of insurance policy.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:50.940
As a result, around $6.7 trillion U.S. dollars
are held in reserve around the world.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.710
The fact that the dollar is the world’s
main reserve currency has given the United

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.620
States significant economic power.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:03.019
It allows the United States to borrow easily,
to recover quickly from economic shocks, and

00:01:03.019 --> 00:01:05.890
to impose sanctions on adversaries.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.650
But power like that comes with some drawbacks,
and there is no guarantee that the dollar

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.380
will keep its status forever.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.080
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.579
Today how the U.S. dollar shapes the world,
and what that means for Americans.

00:01:22.579 --> 00:01:32.200
CBS This Morning: The Federal Reserve is taking
sweeping action to try to protect the economy

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.490
from the coronavirus.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.810
Fed chairman Jerome Powell announced the interest
rate will be drastically cut in one of the

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.390
biggest Fed moves since the 2008 financial
crisis.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.630
ABC World News: China has a huge invested
interest in the American economy because it

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.540
holds almost 25 percent of all foreign-owned
U.S. debts.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.610
DW News: U.S. sanctions against Iran over
its nuclear program were reintroduced...

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.530
Iran’s economy in a free fall, soaring inflation.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.500
Al Jazeera: If push comes to shove, it’s
all..

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:03.009
It's the world reserve currency.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:12.610
SIERRA: So to start off, what is a reserve
currency and why does it matter?

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.990
MALLABY: So, Gabby, I think the first thing
is to explain what a currency is before we

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.910
get to reserve currency.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:24.019
SIERRA: This is Sebastian Mallaby, a senior
fellow for international economics at the

00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:25.019
Council.

00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:29.440
You may remember him from our episode on the
economic side of the COVID pandemic.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.050
MALLABY: A currency has a few different uses.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.090
One is that it's a unit of account.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:40.360
It's a way of putting a measure on what something
might be worth.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.150
The second is, once you've decided what it's
worth, that you should be able to transact.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.610
So transactions are the second idea that you
can go off and buy stuff, with the currency.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:55.620
And a third idea, is that you can use money
as a store of value.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.310
Before you transact, you can save up this
currency, and then transact in the future.

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:05.250
SIERRA: So for those keeping track at home,
money does three basic things.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.880
It lets us agree on prices, it lets us buy
things, and it lets us save up so we can buy

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.170
other things later.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:17.890
Now, the same three things that currency does
for people, reserve currencies do for entire

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.230
countries: pricing, buying, and saving.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:27.450
MALLABY: So literally, a reserve currency
is the currency you hold in reserves.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:34.320
So it's very linked to the last idea I mentioned
of the store of value and in particular, we

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.050
think of reserve banks,I mean, central banks,
global central banks, which hold, currencies

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:46.390
and reserve to protect their economies against
various kinds of financial or economic shock.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:51.380
So a reserve currency really has a couple
of different functions.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:54.519
One is to cover the need for future imports.

00:03:54.519 --> 00:04:00.069
If a country, for example, doesn't have its
own energy supply, needs to import oil, you

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:05.019
might want to hold dollars in reserve to make
sure that you can buy the oil you need in

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:06.060
the future.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:11.409
The second reason central banks hold foreign
currency reserves is because of the possibility

00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:12.819
of a financial crisis.

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:18.829
And there again,dollars are very useful because
most institutions borrow in dollars.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.389
So in a financial crisis, they're going to
need dollars to help them pay back what they

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:24.389
owe.

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:29.270
SIERRA: Central banks have the unique power
to print money, but in many other ways they

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.610
are kinda like a scaled-up version of an ordinary
bank.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.830
Your local bank needs to have enough money
to lend to people if they want to buy a home,

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.470
or if things get bad and they need to take
some out.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.680
A central bank needs to be able to do this
too, but for an entire country.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.259
In order to be prepared for the future, they
need to store up a significant amount of cash

00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:52.199
that they can trust.

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:57.240
Foreign currencies are often a safer bet than
the local currency, especially in countries

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.669
with weak or unstable economies.

00:04:59.669 --> 00:05:03.080
The safest bet of all is...the U.S. dollar.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.699
SIERRA: What role does the US dollar serve
in the global reserve system?

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:12.710
FERGUSON: Well, the U.S. dollar now is the
largest single reserve currency.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:17.270
Economists estimate that approximately 60
percent of foreign reserves around the world

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:19.009
are in US dollars.

00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:20.940
SIERRA: This is Roger Ferguson.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:27.169
He is the President and CEO of TIAA, which
manages over $1 trillion in retirement funds

00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:33.130
for more than 5 million teachers, government
workers and, well, me it turns out.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:38.259
Roger was also Vice Chairman of the Board
of Governors of the Federal Reserve from 1999

00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:39.819
to 2006.

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:44.720
This means he helped determine the supply
of U.S. dollars to the country, and the world.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:51.130
FERGUSON: The euro is about 20 percent the
yen, the Japanese currency is about 5 or 6

00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.960
percent the British pound also, about 5 or
6 percent.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.490
And so the US, by far away, is the most important
reserve currency but it's not the only currency

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.690
that's held in reserve by other economies.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:06.070
The Chinese, I think, would like to see their
currency, the renminbi, become a larger currency

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:09.599
in terms of its holdings as a reserve currency
around the world.

00:06:09.599 --> 00:06:15.080
And yet, you know, in terms of reserves, they
are number four, number five when the list

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.289
had only about 2 percent.

00:06:16.289 --> 00:06:20.110
SIERRA: I can definitely remember going to
Europe at a time when the Euro was way up

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.630
and my money didn't go as far.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:26.080
And you're just like, "Damn, I, I timed (laughs)
this really poorly," Are other countries literally

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.349
buying dollars?

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:33.530
The way that I exchange currency when I visit
a country and where do they buy them from?

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:39.740
MALLABY: It's not quite the same because they're
not collecting lots of $5 bills to put into

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:41.820
the wallet in their belt.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:42.820
SIERRA: Right.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.539
MALLABY: They’re holding huge amounts.

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:51.819
And so they, what they do is they own US treasury
bonds or other kinds of easily traded dollar-

00:06:51.819 --> 00:06:53.860
denominated bonds.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.949
And that could be mortgage bonds as well.

00:06:55.949 --> 00:06:59.300
But you know, US government debt is the most
popular instrument to hold.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.550
SIERRA: And how do US treasury bonds work?

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.889
MALLABY: Well, the government in the United
Stated wants to borrow money to finance the

00:07:06.889 --> 00:07:08.500
U.S. government deficit.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:15.200
So, the treasury sells these bonds, which
are promises to pay back in ten or thirty

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.240
years, or however long the duration of the
bond is.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:27.010
And those bonds are sold in U.S. capital markets
and anybody can buy these bonds and hold the

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:31.573
bonds either in a sort of custodial bank in
America or in another country.

00:07:31.573 --> 00:07:37.210
SIERRA: A treasury bond is essentially a loan
to the U.S. government.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:41.759
The agreement is that you will get your money
back with interest in a set time.

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:46.580
The returns aren’t very high, but they are
considered one of the safest possible investments

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:47.740
in the world.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:52.470
And that safety has led to them being held
more often than all other government bonds

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.599
in the world combined.

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:56.659
But it hasn’t always been that way.

00:07:56.659 --> 00:08:04.100
SIERRA: So how did the U.S. dollar earn its
place as the world's reserve currency or the

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:06.300
biggest, I guess, most prominent reserve currency?

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:10.789
FERGUSON: All of this started at the very
end of the Second World War, when in fact,

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:16.069
we were much more dominant and the world's
economies needed a reserve currency that they

00:08:16.069 --> 00:08:17.069
could all trust.

00:08:17.069 --> 00:08:20.590
They didn't want to use the gold standard
anymore for a variety of reasons.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:24.889
And so, you know, 44 countries got together
here in the United States and said, we are

00:08:24.889 --> 00:08:30.750
going to anoint the U.S. dollar as a reserve
currency and we are going fix the value of

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:33.140
our currencies to the U.S. dollar.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:37.330
And so it's a real reflection of the fact
that at the end of the Second World War, the

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:42.190
world needed the U.S. dollar as a reserve
currency and that has continued now, since

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:43.500
1944 to this very day.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:44.500
SIERRA: Hmm.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:48.260
And the dollar is a lot lighter to carry around
in your pocket, you know, from like a gold

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.260
bar.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.260
FERGUSON: Than an ounce of gold.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.260
Yes, it is.1944 Bretton Woods International
Monetary Conference: At Bretton Woods, New

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:52.260
Hampshire, delegates from 44 allies and associate
countries arrived for the opening of the United

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:53.260
Nations’ monetary and financial conference.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:54.260
Invited by President Roosevelt, for the first
major world financial meeting since the London

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:55.260
conference in 1933, they will work in the
seclusion of this white mountain resort.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.260
IMF: The fund is essential for winning and
preserving peace.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:09:14.270
That is why the representatives are taking
steps to prevent a repetition of the currency

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:25.120
chaos which is usually followed in the wake
of war.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.940
SIERRA: And what helps us maintain this position
now?

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:35.510
MALLABY: I think the first thing to understand
is that there are very strong network effects

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:36.510
with the dollar.

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:41.519
In other words, the more people use the dollar,
the more other people would also like to use

00:09:41.519 --> 00:09:42.519
the dollar as well.

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:46.260
It's a little bit like the English language,
the more people around the world who speak

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:51.410
English, the more it's worth your while to
go take English lessons, if you are a pupil

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.209
at a high school in Indonesia.

00:09:53.209 --> 00:09:57.920
And so it's almost the case that, you know,
the dollar status isn't simply linked to what's

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.390
going on inside the United States.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:04.940
It's also linked to the fact that people in
other countries, whether it's in Latin America

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:10.320
or in Asia, or where have you, are happy to
think in dollars, transact in dollars, borrow

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.730
in dollars, use dollar capital markets.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:17.660
All of that stuff is an incredibly powerful
network effect.

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:20.850
SIERRA: Network effects are fascinating.

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.810
So think of social media platforms, for example.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:30.240
You may not like Facebook or Instagram, personally,
but you may still feel an incentive to remain

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.390
on them because all your friends and family
are there too.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:38.560
If you delete your account, you won’t see
their pictures, and they won’t see yours.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.180
Everyone else uses them, and that keeps you
on, and new users coming in.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:48.380
In part, dollars retain their value because
everyone else expects them to.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:53.700
A company, or a country, that decided to ignore
the dollar would find itself out in the cold.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:56.800
Still, network effects are not the only factor.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.070
FERGUSON: So, the United States doesn't just
have the impression of power.

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:05.019
It is actually the largest single economy
in the world.

00:11:05.019 --> 00:11:10.000
And so, you know, power resides where the
economy itself resides, so to speak.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.920
And the United States is the engine of growth
globally, well, along with China and a few

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:15.920
others.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:20.250
And so, you know, it's not just the impression
that people think we are powerful; we actually

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.320
are powerful because we do have the world's
largest economy.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:29.250
We're also powerful because so many transactions
are denominated in dollars, about half the

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:30.660
world's trade.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:35.950
And so, if the United States decided, for
example,to put sanctions on an economy or

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:40.089
not allow them to have bank accounts in the
United States or freeze those bank accounts,

00:11:40.089 --> 00:11:44.160
it can have a very detrimental impact on that
other economy.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.279
And so there is a reason that people worry
about U.S. sanctions, and the reason is that

00:11:48.279 --> 00:11:53.010
we are a powerful country, and not just simply
the appearance of power, but there's an actuality

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:56.010
of power underneath it as well.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:12:02.500
MALLABY: I think even more important is the
credibility of the legal system, the independence

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:08.150
of the courts, the sense that if you are a
foreigner and you buy a U.S. bond, it's not

00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:12.889
as though a U.S. court will say, " you don't
deserve to be paid back".

00:12:12.889 --> 00:12:16.790
Another very important thing is whether the
United States avoids inflation.

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:21.720
Obviously, if you are lending money in dollars,
you're going to be paid back with future dollars.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.880
And if there's future dollars that devalued
by inflation, you won't be happy with that.

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:31.190
So I think a credible independent Federal
Reserve is extremely important to the status

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:33.380
of the dollar as a reserve currency.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:38.820
SIERRA: The Federal Reserve has a lot of leeway,
and does a very good job of keeping the dollar

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.240
safe and stable.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:45.130
They have the power to increase and decrease
the supply of dollars, even when the public

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:48.279
or a sitting president do not want them to.

00:12:48.279 --> 00:12:53.149
As a result of this independence, transactions
in dollars are seen as the safest around.

00:12:53.149 --> 00:12:58.290
Take a country like Argentina, which has had
huge fluctuations in the value of its currency

00:12:58.290 --> 00:12:59.380
over the years.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:03.709
Let’s say Argentina wants to borrow 10,000
pesos from you.

00:13:03.709 --> 00:13:06.550
And you love Argentina, so you decide to do
it.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:11.529
There’s just one problem, what will those
10,000 pesos be worth when Argentina gives

00:13:11.529 --> 00:13:12.529
them back?

00:13:12.529 --> 00:13:17.399
In 2001 that might have been enough to buy
a car, but a decade earlier, during one of

00:13:17.399 --> 00:13:21.210
the country’s financial crises, it would
have been worth a whole lot less.

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.570
There’s an easy solution.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:26.889
Ask Argentina to price the loan in dollars
instead.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:30.779
Now you can be confident you’ll be getting
the same amount of money back.

00:13:30.779 --> 00:13:36.170
This plays out all over the world, all the
time, whether for loans, barrels of oil, or

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.290
shipping containers full of TVs.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:43.959
And all of that trading and saving in U.S.
dollars has an enormous effect on the U.S.

00:13:43.959 --> 00:13:45.020
economy at home.

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:49.589
SIERRA: So let's look at what our reserve
status does for our own economy.

00:13:49.589 --> 00:13:50.990
What are the good sides?

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:56.510
MALLABY: Well, the good sides are that the
U.S. dollar will be stronger than it would

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:57.880
otherwise be.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:04.430
And so U.S. citizens who have dollars and
get paid in dollars can go on vacation in

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:08.430
Europe, and it would be cheaper than it might
otherwise have been, notwithstanding your

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.690
own ,Gabby,and your poor timing.

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:13.910
MALLABY: Try it again, come to Europe another
time-

00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:14.910
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:15.910
MALLABY:...

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:17.350
you'll, you'll have a nice vacation.

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:21.520
So, U.S. citizens get to go abroad on vacation
a bit more cheaply.

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:26.220
They can also buy imports from stores in the
United Stated a bit cheaper than they would

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:30.060
otherwise have been, if the dollar had been
weaker.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.639
SIERRA: That last one is a biggie.

00:14:32.639 --> 00:14:38.800
As much as American politicians like to insist
on the importance of restoring American manufacturing,

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.720
Americans really, really like to buy cheap
foreign goods.

00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:46.920
When you go to Best Buy, you expect to be
able to get a pretty good flat screen TV for

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:47.920
about $500.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:54.930
Without our reserve status, that TV might
cost hundreds, if not thousands more.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:15:00.259
MALLABY: And then the other one is that the
United States gets to borrow more cheaply

00:15:00.259 --> 00:15:06.190
from foreigners because there is this desire
to hold U.S. assets; foreigners are more willing

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:12.220
to lend money to the United States at lower
interest rates than they would demand otherwise,

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:14.400
because they're getting this sort of safety
advantage.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:18.329
And so the United States can borrow more cheaply
from other countries.

00:15:18.329 --> 00:15:24.420
It is, as the French president Valéry Giscard
d'Estaing once said, "an exorbitant privilege."

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:30.790
The risks in the privileges that if you ever
overuse it and borrow too much, you end up

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:36.279
with a national debt one day that proves very
difficult to sustain, but we haven't reached

00:15:36.279 --> 00:15:37.459
that day yet.

00:15:37.459 --> 00:15:42.080
SIERRA: One of the strangest parts of this
is that during times of tension, when most

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:46.810
countries and individuals would have a hard
time getting a loan, it becomes even easier

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.029
for the United States to do so.

00:15:49.029 --> 00:15:55.170
MALLABY: When you are in a crisis, you want
to hold the safest asset and the dollar is

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.149
perceived as the safest asset.

00:15:57.149 --> 00:16:00.220
And in some sense that perception is self-fulfilling.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:03.170
People expect the dollar to retain its value
in a crisis.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:07.149
And therefore, it does retain its value in
a crisis because money floods into dollar

00:16:07.149 --> 00:16:10.420
assets as being safer than the alternatives.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:18.790
SIERRA: So at times like 9/11 or the 2008
financial crisis, people are looking for dollars.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:20.019
MALLABY: That's right.

00:16:20.019 --> 00:16:25.329
And in fact, that leads to a sort of interesting
paradox, which says something about the nature

00:16:25.329 --> 00:16:27.550
of U.S. power in the world.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:32.360
You can have moments of geopolitical crisis,
like after 9/11, leading up to the preparations

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:39.040
for the Iraq war, where much of the world
is not supportive of U.S. policy; that was

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:40.680
true of the Iraq war, for example.

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:45.639
And they are voting against the United States
at the UN Security Council.

00:16:45.639 --> 00:16:51.079
And yet at the same time, they are buying
U.S. dollars because that's a time of instability

00:16:51.079 --> 00:16:55.519
and there's a flight to safety phenomenon
in currency markets.

00:16:55.519 --> 00:17:01.089
So whilst opposing the U.S. policy in Iraq,
they are in a sense lending money to the United

00:17:01.089 --> 00:17:03.380
States to go and conduct that conflict.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:07.819
And so the United States gets to borrow even
from allies or other countries that are critical

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:09.890
of its policies.

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:15.339
Roger Ferguson was vice chairman of the Federal
Reserve on Sept 11th, and he had to deal with

00:17:15.339 --> 00:17:17.370
this issue first hand.

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:24.079
FERGUSON: That was obviously a moment of phenomenal
tragedy in terms of loss of life in New York

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:26.020
and Washington and in a field in Pennsylvania.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:31.970
It was also a time when the reserve currency
status in the United States was both a strength

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:32.970
and a problem.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:38.460
It was a strength, because as always occurs
during a crisis, people tend to go to the

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:43.750
U.S. markets and in particular, the U.S. treasury
securities as the safe investment; so- called

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:44.750
Safe Haven.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:49.919
What we did see, though, was the fact that
so many debts around the world are denominated

00:17:49.919 --> 00:17:52.940
in dollars, and so much trade is denominated
in the dollars.

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:58.470
That we found many other central banks wanted
to come to us and have access to dollars.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:03.120
And so we had to put in place what's called
a swap arrangement with the ECB and the Bank

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.010
of England, and we had to renew one with Canada.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:10.550
And the same thing I believe has been happening
during the 2020 pandemic as well.

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:14.611
If you have a reserve currency during moments
of crisis, you may find your central bank

00:18:14.611 --> 00:18:20.289
being called upon to be the global banker
and provide your currency through loans or

00:18:20.289 --> 00:18:24.790
through swap arrangements, as they're called,
to others around the world.

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:29.900
SIERRA: As Roger noted a few minutes ago,
our reserve status doesn’t just let us support

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:35.740
allies in moments of crisis, it also lets
us impose painful sanctions on adversaries.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:42.990
MALLABY: Because of the dollar’s incredibly
central role in global finance, global financial

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:48.040
institutions, even if they are, let's say
a Swiss bank, or a Japanese bank, they will

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.130
have an operation in the United States.

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:55.370
It's difficult to be a big international financial
institution and not operate in the United

00:18:55.370 --> 00:18:56.750
States and not operate with dollars.

00:18:56.750 --> 00:19:03.030
And that means that the United Stated has
leverage over pretty much every big financial

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:04.310
institution in the world.

00:19:04.310 --> 00:19:11.159
And if it says, for example, financial institutions
should not lend to Iran or should not lend

00:19:11.159 --> 00:19:17.900
to Russia, or should not facilitate trade
finance with Iran or Russia, or payments for

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:19.360
oil trade, or what have you.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:27.400
It can pretty much freeze those pariah economies
out of global commerce and really exert powerful

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:30.640
sanctions, that would otherwise be difficult
to enforce.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:37.100
So that is another aspect of U.S. power that
derives from the dollar status as a reserve

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:38.100
currency.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:43.060
SIERRA: Our reserve status may be an “exorbitant
privilege”, as that former French president

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:44.060
said.

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:49.350
It makes foreign goods inexpensive,it allows
us to bounce back quickly in times of crisis,

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:53.360
and it empowers us to levy sanctions that
have serious consequences.

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:56.440
But there are some pretty important downsides
too.

00:19:56.440 --> 00:20:00.100
MALLABY: The main disadvantage is sort of
linked to the advantage.

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:05.030
You know, a strong US dollar is nice for U.S.
consumers who get to buy foreign stuff more

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:12.000
cheaply, but not so nice for U.S. producers
that then have to sell their goods competing

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.860
against foreign countries, which have a sort
of cost advantage in production, if the U.S.

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:19.860
dollar is strong; that will hurt US exporters.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:24.220
SIERRA: Here’s the flip side of that $500
TV.

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:29.809
If you want to start a business making TVs
in the United States, you are going to have

00:20:29.809 --> 00:20:30.840
a rough time.

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:35.380
A strong dollar means that you will have to
compete with foreign goods and services that

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:37.660
are cheaper than they otherwise would be.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:43.140
FERGUSON: It may well be that our ability
to run these deficits, very low interest rates,

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:49.080
has ultimately led to the fact that we are
now willing to import more goods in particular

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:50.080
from overseas.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.780
Particularly from the Chinese, but not just
from the Chinese.

00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:57.600
And some people say, well, gee, the lack of
manufacturing in the United States may be

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:02.490
due to the fact that it's been so easy for
us to import goods from other countries, and

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:05.050
that has created some imbalances in our own
economy.

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:14.210
SIERRA: So, what's the connection between
the dollar as reserve currency and our 23.3

00:21:14.210 --> 00:21:16.480
trillion dollars in national debt?

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:21.370
FERGUSON: Well, the link is indirect, but
many people think it's quite real.

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:25.580
Treasury notes, treasury bills, treasury bonds
- those are all debts of the United States

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:28.180
government that we promise to pay back at
some point.

00:21:28.180 --> 00:21:34.120
So, the concern is that the fact that we have
this ready supply of lenders, folks who have

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:38.970
dollars and want to lend it back to us, means
it's easier for us to run this deficit.

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:43.419
And the deficit has gotten larger and larger,
without actually having a big increase in

00:21:43.419 --> 00:21:44.789
interest rates.

00:21:44.789 --> 00:21:46.250
And this is pretty unusual.

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:51.029
And many people think it's the reserve currency
status that has allowed us to be, you know,

00:21:51.029 --> 00:21:55.580
sort of profligate, borrowing more than we
should, living a little bit beyond our means,

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:56.580
perhaps.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:22:00.480
Not taxing to pay for the things that the
U.S. government wants to spend on.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:05.500
The concern is, obviously, over time, not
my generation, but other generations at some

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:10.250
point, will have to pay for this debt that's
outstanding.And that could have a pretty detrimental

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:13.950
impact on our own quality of life here in
the United States eventually.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:18.770
SIERRA: From this perspective the United States
is a bit like a person with a very good credit

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:23.390
score who has a lot of credit card debt at
a very low interest rate.

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:28.380
Some economists feel that it has worked well
for many decades and will continue to do so.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:32.799
Others tend to think a debt is a debt, and
that it will eventually hurt us to keep borrowing

00:22:32.799 --> 00:22:34.419
so much money.

00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:39.860
Amid this debate, there’s one country that
comes up time and again, China.

00:22:39.860 --> 00:22:44.330
SIERRA: So, China owns more US debt than any
other country, right?

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:47.380
So, some people express a lot of alarm about
this.

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:49.150
What's the real story there?

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:52.500
FERGUSON: The real story is more complicated
than, I know.

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:58.241
It's a stunning thought that the story's more
complicated than the headlines.

00:22:58.241 --> 00:23:04.130
So, the reason that China has come to have
so much of U.S. dollar - denominated securities,

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:10.850
particularly treasury bonds and bills, is
that their economy depends very much on exporting

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:12.549
goods to the United States.

00:23:12.549 --> 00:23:16.330
And so, we are in what one might call a symbiotic
relationship.

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:19.490
Both sides sort of benefit from this relationship.

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:24.630
The Chinese have been able to build up their
economy, mainly through manufacturing, taking

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:29.760
many hundreds of millions, if not billions,
of Chinese out of abject poverty.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:34.320
And the United States has been able to benefit,
if one thinks of that, by getting, you know,

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:39.880
relatively inexpensive, relatively high quality
goods made in China and paying for them with

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:44.179
dollars that we can print as many as we want,
and the Chinese are willing to hold them.

00:23:44.179 --> 00:23:49.610
The other concern, by the way, is a fear that
at some point the Chinese, because we have,

00:23:49.610 --> 00:23:55.410
again, a complex relationship with them, may
decide, well, we don't want to keep buying

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:59.270
all these government securities, this debt
that the United States is pumping out every

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:00.270
year.

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:05.260
And then, you know, there's some anxiety that
that will affect the market for U.S. treasury

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:06.260
securities.

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:11.440
And, you know, effectively, the concern is
that the Chinese can use their reserves in

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:14.630
a way that can be quite adversarial to U.S.
interests.

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:20.110
But people ignore that; if they were gonna
do that, the value of the debt that they themselves

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:24.980
hold, the Chinese hold, would go down, and
so they would suffer some of the pain as well.

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:27.390
________________
SIERRA: So when someone says China could call

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:32.620
in our debt tomorrow and ruin us, that doesn't
strike you as a particularly accurate way

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:33.620
of looking at it?

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:37.650
FERGUSON: It's not a completely accurate way
of looking at it because, you know, they are

00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:40.740
holding a debt for their own good (laughs)
reasons.

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:45.510
Which is it allows them to maintain this export
oriented manufacturing sector that has been

00:24:45.510 --> 00:24:50.330
so central to their financial success, and
economic success over the last thirty or forty

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:51.330
years.

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:56.330
SIERRA: Yes, we’ve grown dependent on China
buying our treasuries, but China also depends

00:24:56.330 --> 00:24:57.870
on us to buy their goods.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:25:03.440
It’s not a situation that either country
can pull out of without doing harm to itself.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:05.840
But things do change with time.

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:10.240
For millennia, gold was the most reliable
currency across the world.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:14.080
As empires came and went, so did their currencies.

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:18.169
If you asked a British citizen about the pound
in the 19th century, they might have thought

00:25:18.169 --> 00:25:20.570
that it would remain the world’s currency
forever.

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:27.240
When we look to the future of reserve currencies,
do you think the dollar could ever lose ground

00:25:27.240 --> 00:25:28.250
on its status?

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:31.810
FERGUSON: So, the answer is the dollar could
certainly lose ground on its status, the way

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:32.810
you phrase it.

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:34.970
In fact, we have over time lost ground.

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:40.649
Notice that I said that now about 60 percent
of foreign reserves are held in U.S. dollars.

00:25:40.649 --> 00:25:44.800
Well, back in the 40s and the 50s, that number
was clearly much higher.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:50.490
So the answer is, yes, we already have lost
some of our positioning as a reserve currency.And

00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:54.160
I would expect over time that that was likely
to continue.

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:59.690
SIERRA: The United States is slowly losing
ground, but most economists think that its

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:02.740
position is likely to continue for the foreseeable
future.

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:07.600
Still, that hasn’t stopped many from speculating
about the potential for another currency to

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:09.250
displace the U.S. dollar.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:13.649
And some of these theorists think that it
won’t be another national currency at all,

00:26:13.649 --> 00:26:17.460
but rather cryptocurrency - digital assets
like Bitcoin.

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:23.730
Al Jazeera: It’s bitcoin season again; at
the time of the year when the world’s most

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:29.700
famous and least understood currency explodes
in value seemingly for no reasons at all.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:35.090
CNN: The world’s richest man now investing
1.5 billion dollars worth of his company’s

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:36.540
money in the crypto currency.

00:26:36.540 --> 00:26:42.290
Kitco News: A former Canadian prime minister
Steven Hart recently said that he sees a potential

00:26:42.290 --> 00:26:44.669
for the crypto to become a reserve currency.

00:26:44.669 --> 00:26:45.669
MALLABY: Yes.

00:26:45.669 --> 00:26:48.659
Going back, there's been two kinds of money,
really.

00:26:48.659 --> 00:26:53.529
There's been money created by national governments,
but there's also been commodities, notably

00:26:53.529 --> 00:26:56.330
gold, a little bit silver.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:27:01.600
And through history, there have been periods
when people have lost faith in governments

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:07.410
issuing money, and they've bought gold as
a protection against government-created currencies

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:08.410
going wrong.

00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:10.980
Bitcoin is sort of the new version of that.

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:13.720
It's a non-government kind of currency.

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:21.130
The argument of the Bitcoin enthusiasts is
that it's not safe to keep your money in dollars

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:27.059
because there might be inflation and you never
know the dollar might be devalued by inflation.

00:27:27.059 --> 00:27:33.820
Therefore, you want some alternative asset
that is absolutely going to retain its value.

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:40.340
And they say, well, Bitcoin could be that
asset because it's supply is algorithmically

00:27:40.340 --> 00:27:41.340
capped.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:45.370
You can't have more than a certain amount
of Bitcoin, and therefore you can't print

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:47.750
tons of it and destroy its value.

00:27:47.750 --> 00:27:55.530
Now, for various reasons, this Bitcoin proposition
doesn't quite convince everybody, not least

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:56.530
me.

00:27:56.530 --> 00:27:57.530
I'm trying to be tactful about this.

00:27:57.530 --> 00:28:01.870
You know, if you're trying to argue that the
dollar's value might be unstable and you're

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:03.789
saying, well, Bitcoin is better.

00:28:03.789 --> 00:28:07.179
The fact that you've had these incredible
bubbles and crashes in Bitcoin in the last

00:28:07.179 --> 00:28:10.240
five years is not encouraging to that thesis.

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:16.799
And although the supply of Bitcoin is algorithmically
capped, most algorithmic systems have been

00:28:16.799 --> 00:28:19.450
susceptible to hacking in the past.

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:24.600
And I'm not sure that I trust some anonymously
created code more than I trust the Federal

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:26.740
Reserve Bank of the United States.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:31.890
Most of the enthusiasm for Bitcoin is people
who just basically don't trust the government.

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:37.850
But there could be another type of enthusiasm,
which is people who don't like the U.S. dominance

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:38.850
of the system.

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:46.269
They're more about being anti-American than
being anti-government.So, that sort of resentment

00:28:46.269 --> 00:28:52.289
of the dollar, which you see today in countries
like Russia, which are the object of U.S.

00:28:52.289 --> 00:28:58.360
sanctions, and those sanctions are very much
made more powerful by the dollar’s global

00:28:58.360 --> 00:28:59.360
status.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:04.669
You see pushes to create alternatives to the
dollar from countries that don't like U.S.

00:29:04.669 --> 00:29:09.630
power, and indeed, the Chinese efforts to
advance the renminbi as an international currency

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:14.769
are very much linked to a sense that the United
States just has too much power in the system

00:29:14.769 --> 00:29:17.890
because of the dollar status as the global
reserves currency.

00:29:17.890 --> 00:29:22.750
SIERRA: Which brings us to the other currency
that some think could eventually eclipse the

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:24.899
dollar: the Chinese Renminbi.

00:29:24.899 --> 00:29:30.410
MALLABY: In order to have a reserve currency,
one thing you've got to provide is the ability

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:36.899
for foreigners to easily buy bonds in your
currency, and to feel confident that having

00:29:36.899 --> 00:29:41.030
bought those bonds, they can sell them and
get a fair price for them.

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:45.419
And they won't be subject to all kinds of
restraints and controls.

00:29:45.419 --> 00:29:50.420
And China's capital markets simply are not
free enough and open enough yet for that to

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:51.420
be the case.

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:55.910
It is moving in that direction in the last
few years; China has gone quite a way to creating

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:59.440
an impressive, corporate bond market, for
example.

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:04.750
And that's a step in the direction of having
a credible claim on being a reserve currency,

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:08.000
but there is quite a long road for China to
go.

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:12.519
FERGUSON: It is certainly true that the Chinese,
I think, would like to see their currency,

00:30:12.519 --> 00:30:18.419
the renminbi, become a larger currency in
terms of its holdings as a reserve currency

00:30:18.419 --> 00:30:19.779
around the world.

00:30:19.779 --> 00:30:24.740
I think that for them creates a sense of belonging
in the international community, and frankly,

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:26.490
a sense of prestige.

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:30.169
After all, they are by many measures the second
largest economy in the world.

00:30:30.169 --> 00:30:33.279
And so there is, I think, bragging rights
as far as they're concerned.

00:30:33.279 --> 00:30:39.279
I think, more importantly, the real question
is whether or not, you know, trading partners,

00:30:39.279 --> 00:30:44.279
others believe that it's very important to
maintain, you know, good relationships with

00:30:44.279 --> 00:30:48.830
China and whether or not they see that as
being a question of maintaining relationships

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:53.660
with China versus the United States, or China
in addition to the United States.

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:58.029
And so I think this whole issue of reserve
currency status is a little bit in the eyes

00:30:58.029 --> 00:31:03.170
of the Chinese, a sign of prestige, and it's
a sign of being respected as an important

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:05.440
part of the global system.

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:09.730
From the standpoint of the United States,
I think, we would very much like to understand

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:13.360
how China wants to participate in the global
economy.

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:16.790
And I would observe that we want to make sure
that they want to participate in a way that's

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:22.299
respectful of the values of others, respectful,
frankly, of democracy.

00:31:22.299 --> 00:31:27.370
SIERRA: China certainly has the economic heft
to challenge the United States on reserve

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:33.370
currency status, but it may lack some of the
other elements that lead to trust in a currency.

00:31:33.370 --> 00:31:38.500
One problem is that as an authoritarian nation,
China doesn’t always play by the same rules

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:40.370
as the Western world.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:44.730
China has been accused of dumping products,
manipulating the value of its currency, and

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:47.920
exerting strict controls over foreign investments.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:53.040
Taken together, these factors make investors
worry that Beijing will prioritize its own

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:57.190
goals over the safety of their investment.

00:31:57.190 --> 00:32:04.600
So Do you think that the power of our reserve
status comes with a sort of, you know, global

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:05.600
responsibility?

00:32:05.600 --> 00:32:09.549
I guess what I'm trying to ask is whether
it makes us a kind of caretaker for the global

00:32:09.549 --> 00:32:10.549
economy.

00:32:10.549 --> 00:32:12.580
MALLABY: Yes, we are.

00:32:12.580 --> 00:32:19.330
The Federal Reserve proved after 2008 how
important it was to stabilize not just the

00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:23.779
U.S. financial system, but other countries
as well, because those other countries use

00:32:23.779 --> 00:32:25.570
dollars so much.

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:31.080
It's important that the Federal Reserve be
ready to provide emergency infusions of dollars

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:32.620
when they're needed.

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:38.049
And that's what the Fed did, with its so-called
swap lines in 2009.

00:32:38.049 --> 00:32:44.580
And that readiness is an aspect of sort of
the U.S. continued guardianship of the global

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:45.580
system.

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:50.420
FERGUSON: So, you know, there are people who
think we have the obligation, because we have

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:55.380
a reserve currency, to be a bit of a caretaker
for the global economy.

00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:58.850
My view is the most important thing we can
do for the global economy is to make sure

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:03.230
that our economy is functioning smoothly,
that we keep inflation under control, that

00:33:03.230 --> 00:33:06.590
we grow our potential, that we maintain maximum
employment.

00:33:06.590 --> 00:33:10.610
That is, I think, the ultimate obligation
that we have here in the United States, and

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:13.679
then everything else will, I think, fall into
place over time.

00:33:13.679 --> 00:33:21.840
SIERRA: So last question, and it's a little
bit of a toughie, but as a normal person,

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:26.760
just going about minding my own business and
trying to make it in the world today, how

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:30.539
does the dollar as a reserve affect my life?

00:33:30.539 --> 00:33:36.179
MALLABY: For a regular U.S. citizen, the U.S.
dollar status as a reserve currency means

00:33:36.179 --> 00:33:40.940
one thing for you, which is the dollars that
you earn are more valuable in the world, and

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:45.130
you can buy more foreign staff and go on more
foreign vacations.

00:33:45.130 --> 00:33:51.169
And secondly, it means that your country is
more secure and more powerful because that

00:33:51.169 --> 00:33:56.730
dollar status means that in a crunch, the
United States can borrow the money it needs.

00:33:56.730 --> 00:34:02.049
It means that in a geopolitical standoff,
the United States can impose sanctions on

00:34:02.049 --> 00:34:05.520
other countries and they will have more force.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:10.520
And just the sheer importance of the dollar
in the global system gives the United States

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:18.690
diplomatic clout that is of valuable asset
for the nation.

